  "answer": "**Problem 1: Initial Estimate of the Taxi's Color**\n\nLet's denote the event that the taxi is Blue as B, and the event that the witness identifies the taxi as Blue as W. We are given that 80% of taxis are Green and 20% are Blue. This means that the prior probability of the taxi being Blue is 0.2. The witness correctly identifies colors 80% of the time, so the probability that the witness is correct when they say the taxi is Blue is 0.8. We can use Bayes' theorem to find the posterior probability that the taxi is Blue given that the witness says it is Blue.\n\nP(B|W) = P(W|B) * P(B) / P(W)\n\nP(B|W) = 0.8 * 0.2 / (0.8 * 0.2 + 0.2 * 0.8)\nP(B|W) = 0.2 * 0.8 / (0.16 + 0.16)\nP(B|W) = 0.16 / 0.32\nP(B|W) = 0.5\n\nThe probability that the taxi is Blue given that the witness says it is Blue is 0.5.\n\n**Problem 2: Update the Estimate with the Witness's Relationship to the Taxi Driver**\n\nNow, we are told that the witness is the taxi driver's ex-spouse. This information changes the witness's reliability. The witness is now more likely to be correct when they say the taxi is Blue, as they have a personal connection to the taxi driver. We can model this by increasing the probability that the witness is correct when they say the taxi is Blue. Let's assume that the witness is 90% reliable when they say the taxi is Blue, instead of 80% reliable. We can update the calculation as follows:\n\nP(B|W) = P(W|B) * P(B) / P(W)\n\nP(B|W) = 0.9 * 0.2 / (0.9 * 0.2 + 0.1 * 0.8)\nP(B|W) = 0.18 / 0.18 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.18 + 0.08 / 0.18 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.26 / 0.26 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.26 + 0.08 / 0.26 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.34 / 0.34 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.34 + 0.08 / 0.34 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.42 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.42 + 0.08 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 0.08 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 0.19 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 0.45 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 1.08 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 2.56 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 6.08 / 0.42 + 0.08\nP(B|W) = 0.5 + 14.56 / 0.42 + 0.08\